  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/loricaria-lata-2/",
      "html": "<p>Of the larger-growing, mouthbrooding whiptail catfishes of the genus Loricaria, only the very widespread L. simillima is in the trade from time to time. The others (there are 17 recognized species plus several scientifically undetermined ones) are absolute top rarities. L. lata has now been received from Brazil. This species grows 20-25 cm long.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/loricaria-lata-2/",
      "html": "<p>It originates from the drainage of the Rio Tocantins, Brazil.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "equipment",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/loricaria-lata-2/",
      "html": "<p>A sandy bottom is therefore useful for care, even if the fish then look in close-up as if they had white spot disease (Ichthyophthirius). The animals are perfectly healthy.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/loricaria-lata-2/",
      "html": "<p>Among themselves the fish are peaceful. However, they are somewhat skittish and try to hide in the bottom when disturbed.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/loricaria-lata-2/",
      "html": "<p>Like many mouthbrooding loricariids, this species is polychromatic, i.e. within one population there are quite differently marked animals. Some are almost completely black, others rather bright with black bands, and there are all conceivable intermediate stages; depending on mood the animals can also change color.</p>"
    }
  ],
